WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.570
If you look at a salt crystal you see the atoms repeating at fixed intervals in space.

2
00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:13.060
But can a piece of matter do the same thing in time — can it oscillate in the same rhythm forever, consuming no energy at all?

3
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:21.510
The physicist who asked that question proved his own answer impossible; then others shifted it one step sideways and realised it in the laboratory.

4
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:36.720
Take a single grain of salt and blow it up large enough, and what you see has a patience about it that is almost unsettling.

5
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:44.370
Sodium and chlorine atoms are laid out like a three-dimensional chessboard, in rows that follow one another without ever losing step.

6
00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:52.180
The distance from one atom to its neighbour is always the same; measure it once and you have measured the distance at the far end of the grain as well.

7
00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.150
And yet this arrangement is written down nowhere.

8
00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:04.760
There is no such plan in the water the salt was dissolved in; there every point is like every other, no position enjoys any privilege.

9
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:09.850
As the water evaporates, the atoms pick a place.

10
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:17.500
The moment they pick it, the uniformity of space is broken: from then on, here and half a nanometre away are different from each other.

11
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:24.190
Physicists call this spontaneous symmetry breaking, and the word spontaneous carries the whole weight of the thing.

12
00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:28.160
The laws of nature favour no point in space.

13
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.370
But matter does.

14
00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:36.100
The law stays neutral while matter takes sides, picks a pattern and locks onto it.

15
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:42.470
That is exactly what a crystal is: an order that repeats itself through space and that nobody dictated.

16
00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:45.640
It also has a measurable signature.

17
00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:52.170
Shine X-rays on a crystal and on the detector behind it you see not a blurred smudge but needle-sharp spots.

18
00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:56.940
That sharpness is the proof of how meticulous the repetition is.

19
00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:09.710
In the autumn of 2012, Frank Wilczek, working at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology and one of the winners of the 2004 Nobel Prize in Physics, put an uncomfortable question to this very familiar picture.

20
00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:16.720
Ever since relativity we have not thought of time as a separate ledger from space; the two are threads of the same fabric.

21
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.970
And just as the laws favour no point in space, they favour no moment in time.

22
00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:25.460
An equation that holds today holds tomorrow.

23
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:31.990
What this symmetry corresponds to in physics is nothing ordinary: it is the conservation of energy itself.

24
00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:34.360
Wilczek's question was this.

25
00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:40.570
If matter can break the uniformity of space on its own, why should it not break the uniformity of time?

26
00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:46.300
Could a piece of matter, by itself, keep a rhythm in time the way it keeps a pattern in space?

27
00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:54.270
Something has to be separated out here, because the description of a thing that repeats in time sounds boringly ordinary on first hearing.

28
00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.440
A pendulum repeats.

29
00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.650
A heart beats.

30
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.180
A planet turns.

31
00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:10.310
But all of these were either set going by somebody's first push or are slowly burning through a store of energy; leave them alone and they all stop.

32
00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.160
What Wilczek wanted was far harder.

33
00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:19.050
Imagine a piece of matter that has sunk to the lowest state physics allows, the lowest energy state.

34
00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:23.020
Nothing left to give, nowhere left to be cooled to.

35
00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.870
And this matter still does not stop.

36
00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:28.240
It oscillates.

37
00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:35.250
Its motion, in other words, is not the last writhing of some leftover energy; it is stillness itself.

38
00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:41.940
For such matter the calmest possible condition is not to sit motionless, but to stir with a definite period.

39
00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:47.350
This sounds like something forbidden, and it matters to understand why it was thought forbidden.

40
00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.680
It has nothing to do with perpetual motion machines.

41
00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:55.930
You cannot draw energy out of such matter; you cannot run a wire from it and light a bulb.

42
00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:59.900
Nothing flows out, nothing is used up inside.

43
00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:09.790
In fact we already know something that resembles this from a distance: the current circulating in a superconducting ring goes round, with no battery at all, in principle forever.

44
00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:13.920
What Wilczek proposed was to carry that idea one step further.

45
00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:25.410
His concrete example was close to it as well, charged particles confined to a ring with magnetic flux threading through it, a system whose lowest-energy arrangement is not sitting still but travelling around the ring.

46
00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:34.340
Together with Alfred Shapere he also designed a cousin of this in classical physics: a system in which having zero speed at minimum energy is forbidden.

47
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.190
The name of the idea was irresistible.

48
00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.240
Time crystal.

49
00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:44.490
But physicists are drawn less to beautiful names than to ideas that look like contraband.

50
00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:47.820
And this idea looked exactly like contraband.

51
00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.270
If there is a rhythm even in the lowest state, that rhythm is a clock.

52
00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:57.840
And a clock nobody wound and nobody feeds is the kind of thing that makes a physicist's nose itch.

53
00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:03.730
The most reliable sign that an idea is being taken seriously is that someone sets out to demolish it.

54
00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:08.500
The time crystal sat that examination both quickly and harshly.

55
00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.670
The first blow came in 2013.

56
00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:23.960
Patrick Bruno, working at the European Synchrotron Radiation Facility in Grenoble, went after the proposed ring model and showed that the state with the circulating current was not in fact the lowest-energy state.

57
00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:29.850
Then he generalised his objection: the ground state of a system cannot rotate of its own accord.

58
00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:41.660
But the real blow came in 2015, from Haruki Watanabe and Masaki Oshikawa at the University of Tokyo, and this time the objection was aimed not at a single model but at the definition of the idea.

59
00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:50.110
The heart of the theorem they proved will feel maddeningly familiar to anyone who knows a little quantum mechanics: the ground state is an energy eigenstate,

60
00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:55.040
and the measurable properties of an energy eigenstate do not change with time.

61
00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.210
It is stationary because it is stationary.

62
00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:01.380
The same holds for a system in thermal equilibrium.

63
00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:12.870
So in a closed system, under time-independent laws, and provided the interactions are not very long-ranged, you cannot see a persistent oscillation in equilibrium spread across the whole system.

64
00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:16.520
Measure what you like, what you get is a flat line.

65
00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:22.250
Within three years the idea seemed to have been buried by a sentence sharper than the one that gave birth to it.

66
00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:27.340
Impossibility theorems usually work like this in physics; they close a door.

67
00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:31.790
But read carefully, they also tell you which door they closed.

68
00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:36.880
Watanabe and Oshikawa's theorem did not say that the idea was silly.

69
00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.050
It said that it could not happen in equilibrium.

70
00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:43.860
And equilibrium is a very small room in the universe.

71
00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:49.430
Everything we live through, from our cells to the stars, happens outside that room.

72
00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.960
The way around the ban came out of this.

73
00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:57.530
What happens if, instead of leaving the system alone, you start prodding it without pause?

74
00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:03.100
You send it laser or microwave pulses at a definite period, at regular intervals.

75
00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:09.150
Such a system is no longer in its ground state, so it has fallen outside the scope of the theorem.

76
00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:14.560
But something more interesting also happens: the symmetry to be broken changes.

77
00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:20.930
You no longer have the unbroken uniformity of time; the laws now repeat themselves only once every period.

78
00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:25.220
And breaking that discrete symmetry means something very precise.

79
00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:30.470
You prod the system a thousand times a second, and it answers five hundred times.

80
00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:34.280
It settles into a rhythm at twice the period of the drive.

81
00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:38.730
And here the resemblance to space becomes tighter than it was at the start.

82
00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:43.340
A crystal takes continuous space and leaves a discrete lattice behind it.

83
00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:49.070
A driven time crystal takes the discrete tempo of the drive and leaves a coarser tempo behind it.

84
00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:51.920
But there were two serious obstacles.

85
00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:53.970
The first is this.

86
00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:57.300
Doubling a period is not a rare event.

87
00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:03.190
A child on a swing, a dripping tap, certain electronic circuits all do it.

88
00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:07.480
What it takes for something to count as a phase of matter is rigidity.

89
00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:15.610
Shift the period of the drive a little, make the pulses imperfect, and an ordinary period doubler will either drift or start following you.

90
00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:23.740
A time crystal locks on; even if you move the parameters within a certain range, it goes on standing at exactly twice.

91
00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:26.910
That is what separates a coincidence from an order.

92
00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:30.080
The second obstacle was more insidious.

93
00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:38.690
If you prod a piece of matter without pause, you are pumping energy into it without pause, and a many-particle system swallows that energy, heats up,

94
00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:44.100
and in the end turns into an indescribable mush at what is effectively infinite temperature.

95
00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:46.470
No order survives there.

96
00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:50.920
The rescue came from a phenomenon known as many-body localisation.

97
00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:57.930
If there is enough disorder and the right kind of interaction, the system cannot spread energy around inside itself.

98
00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.460
It refuses to heat up.

99
00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:12.270
In 2016, Vedika Khemani and three colleagues worked out the phase diagram of a disordered, periodically driven spin chain and found a phase in which the spins stubbornly turned in a period-doubled pattern.

100
00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:18.000
Another team that same year gave this state the name Floquet time crystal.

101
00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.210
Now there was a recipe.

102
00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:29.620
The recipe was turned into a meal in two separate laboratories in March 2017, and the two were published side by side in the same issue of the same journal.

103
00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:36.470
At the University of Maryland, Christopher Monroe's team used 10 ytterbium ions lined up in an electromagnetic trap.

104
00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:47.960
With alternating laser pulses they flipped the spins and forced them to interact with one another, added disorder on purpose, and saw the system's magnetisation oscillating at twice the period of the drive,

105
00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:52.250
staying locked to that tempo even when they shifted the drive slightly.

106
00:09:52.250 --> 00:10:03.580
At Harvard University, Mikhail Lukin's team caught the same signature in an entirely different setting, in the crowded and disordered world of nitrogen-vacancy defects scattered through a diamond lattice,

107
00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:06.110
using microwaves.

108
00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:13.280
The same order, in two systems, one a chain of ions hanging almost in vacuum, the other a solid stone.

109
00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.770
Even so, the case was not closed.

110
00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:22.500
These oscillations lived only a few dozen cycles, which is to say milliseconds.

111
00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:31.270
The critics' objection was fair: perhaps these were not a real phase, but an intermediate state stuck for a long while on the way to thermal equilibrium.

112
00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:36.520
You cannot prove that something lasts forever with an experiment that lasts a millisecond.

113
00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:40.810
The step that largely closed the argument came in November 2021.

114
00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:48.460
Teams from Stanford, Google and Max Planck used 20 qubits as spins on Google's quantum processor, Sycamore.

115
00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:57.070
The advantage of a programmable machine was this: you can start the system from hundreds of different initial states, sweep the parameters one by one,

116
00:10:57.070 --> 00:11:02.160
and use echo techniques to separate the hardware's own decay from the physics itself.

117
00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:09.650
What they showed was that this order was not the trick of one lucky initial state, but was soaked through the system's entire energy spectrum.

118
00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:15.860
Even so, the lifetime was limited by the machine's own decay time; hundreds of cycles.

119
00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:19.510
So at the end of 2021 this is what was in hand.

120
00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:27.480
A way around the theorem had been found, the signature had been seen on very different platforms, most of the objections had been answered.

121
00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:30.810
The remaining problem was embarrassingly simple.

122
00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:33.820
These things lived a very short time.

123
00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:39.390
A millisecond can be a lifetime for physics; for a material it is nothing.

124
00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:44.320
That wall came down in February 2024, and by several times over.

125
00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:51.170
At TU Dortmund, Alex Greilich's team was working on a special semiconductor made of indium gallium arsenide.

126
00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:54.980
They illuminated the sample with continuous light.

127
00:11:54.980 --> 00:12:02.310
The light was aligning the electron spins, and the electrons were passing that alignment on to the spins of the atomic nuclei around them.

128
00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:11.400
The nuclear spins thus became a kind of reservoir, and this electron and nucleus pair began to oscillate on its own, with nobody dictating a period to it.

129
00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:14.410
The system chose the frequency.

130
00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:18.220
This was a different kind of thing from the earlier experiments.

131
00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:25.070
In driven time crystals the rhythm is half the tempo coming from outside; you set the beat and the matter halves it.

132
00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:34.320
Here there was no tempo coming from outside, only uninterrupted light; the symmetry being broken was not the discrete but the continuous uniformity of time.

133
00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:37.650
This is called a continuous time crystal.

134
00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:45.140
The system is open, energy enters as light and leaves as heat, so it too is outside the room of the old theorem.

135
00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:48.790
And the lifetime: at least 40 minutes.

136
00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:53.080
About 10 million times longer than anything shown until that day.

137
00:12:53.080 --> 00:13:00.250
What is more, 40 minutes was not where the crystal ended but where the measurement ended; it could probably have gone on much longer.

138
00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:06.620
When the lifetime of something goes from milliseconds to minutes, the question being asked changes too.

139
00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:10.910
It is no longer whether the thing exists, but what it is good for.

140
00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:16.640
The second threshold was about dimension, and it was crossed in January 2026.

141
00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:28.930
A team led by Eric Switzer observed a two-dimensional discrete time crystal, using 144 qubits of IBM's 156-qubit Heron r2 processor in the shape of a decorated hexagonal lattice.

142
00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:33.060
To see why this matters you have to go back to that heating problem.

143
00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:45.030
It was seriously doubtful whether many-body localisation, which saves the day in one dimension, could survive in two; in two dimensions there are far more routes by which energy can escape and disperse.

144
00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:56.200
The team did not only see the oscillation, they mapped the entire phase diagram: a spin glass region on one side, an ergodic region where everything races to equilibrium on the other,

145
00:13:56.200 --> 00:14:00.010
and the time crystal phase in between.

146
00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:03.980
They compared their results with tensor network calculations.

147
00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:07.950
So this order is not a by-product of a one-dimensional model.

148
00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:14.480
And in March 2026, 14 years after the idea was born, the first real job arrived.

149
00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:24.210
Ashok Ajoy of the University of California, Berkeley, Paul Schindler of the Max Planck Institute for the Physics of Complex Systems and their colleagues turned a time crystal,

150
00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:30.100
built from carbon nuclear spins inside diamond, into a measuring instrument.

151
00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:32.310
The logic is this.

152
00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:37.400
If the strongest feature of this phase is its rigidity, then use the rigidity.

153
00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:42.010
You place the crystal inside a weak, oscillating magnetic field.

154
00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:52.220
When the frequency of the field matches the crystal's own frequency the crystal is not disrupted; on the contrary, its lifetime grows longer, it makes food of the signal.

155
00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:58.110
The result is a very narrow-band detector that burns only at the frequency it is tuned to.

156
00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:02.240
The range it works in is between half a kilohertz and 50 kilohertz.

157
00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:07.010
That band is precisely the awkward region for many other quantum sensors.

158
00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:17.540
And what sets the sensitivity is not the fine tuning of the interactions but the lifetime of the crystal; and it is robust against imperfections in the pulses and inconsistencies in the sample,

159
00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:20.070
because the order itself is rigid.

160
00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:27.880
The thing that was considered a nuisance for years, the crowd interacting with itself, here becomes the operating principle of the instrument.

161
00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:31.850
A similar door was opened at Aalto University in Finland.

162
00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:44.780
Jere Mäkinen's team sent radio waves into superfluid helium-3 at temperatures very close to absolute zero, creating excitations called magnons; these excitations organised themselves and set up a time crystal.

163
00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:55.150
What the team did that was new was to connect this crystal to a mechanical oscillator, making it talk for the first time with an apparatus outside itself, and to tune its properties by that route.

164
00:15:55.150 --> 00:16:01.520
The analogy they draw is with the optomechanical techniques used in gravitational wave detectors.

165
00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:09.490
Sensitive detectors and quantum memory are the possibilities this work points towards; not devices already built, but roads opened.

166
00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:13.140
There is also an example you can see.

167
00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:24.790
In September 2025, Hanqing Zhao and Ivan Smalyukh of the University of Colorado Boulder showed that when liquid crystals squeezed between glass plates coated with dye molecules are driven with light,

168
00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:30.360
thousands of defects that behave like particles form, and their pattern repeats for hours.

169
00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:35.770
This pattern can be seen with a microscope, and under the right conditions even with the naked eye.

170
00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:43.420
The uses the researchers propose are light-activated anti-counterfeiting marks and data storage; these are at the level of proposals for now.

171
00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:48.350
The result that best describes where the field stands today comes from this very month.

172
00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:58.560
The Dortmund team, in a gallium arsenide sample doped with indium and silicon, at around minus 270 degrees Celsius, set up separate time crystals;

173
00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:04.610
at the centre of each is a localised electron and roughly 1 million nuclear spins talking to it.

174
00:17:04.610 --> 00:17:07.140
What they see is this.

175
00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:13.350
Two of these oscillators lock to the same frequency despite being as much as 40 micrometres apart.

176
00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:17.800
That distance is more than a thousand times the length of a single oscillator.

177
00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:24.330
The bond between them is not mechanical; it is carried by the drift through the crystal of electrons whose spins are aligned.

178
00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:34.380
The researchers draw the comparison themselves: in 1665 Christiaan Huygens noticed that two pendulum clocks hanging from the same beam fell into step with each other.

179
00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:38.030
In this 14-year story nobody refuted a theorem.

180
00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:48.240
Watanabe and Oshikawa's proof still stands; rather than demolish it, physicists changed the room, stepping out of equilibrium into driven and open systems.

181
00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:53.490
Nor does free energy come out of a time crystal; they all eat either light or pulses.

182
00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:55.700
What comes out is subtler.

183
00:17:55.700 --> 00:18:06.230
It is that, just as there is repetition in space, a repetition in time can be chosen spontaneously by matter, which is to say that there is one more kind of order in the world.

184
00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:13.080
Whether what follows turns into real laboratory instruments or stays a beautiful demonstration is still an open question.

185
00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:21.530
But what is argued about now is no longer whether matter that keeps a beat when nobody is counting is possible; it is what we are going to lock it to.

186
00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:25.500
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

187
00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.990
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
